  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Ochre gillgobbler (Hypomyces lateritius) is a parasitic fungus in the Hypocreaceae family that infects the gills of other fungi, transforming them into a mummified state. This species is found in the moist woodlands and forests of New England, where it plays an ecological role in fungal decomposition.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Host Specificity",
      "description": "Primarily parasitizes russula and brittlegill fungi (Russula species), which become distorted and mummified",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Ochre Coloration",
      "description": "Displays distinctive ochre to rusty-yellow coloring on infected gill surfaces and fruiting body exterior",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Distorted Morphology",
      "description": "Host mushrooms become warped and lose their normal gill structure, appearing swollen and grotesque",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Ochre gillgobbler thrives in moist hardwood and mixed forests throughout New England, appearing most commonly from summer through fall on the forest floor.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The name 'gillgobbler' reflects its parasitic feeding behavior—this fungus essentially consumes the gills of its host mushroom, leaving behind a zombie-like mummified fruiting body.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
